== Chapter XI

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[The #smallcaps[third] direction proposed: Load your conscience with the guilt of the troubling disorder — The ways and means by which this may be done — The #smallcaps[fourth] direction: Vehement desire for deliverance — The #smallcaps[fifth]: Some disorders rooted deeply in men’s natural temperaments — Considerations on such disorders; ways of dealing with them — The #smallcaps[sixth] direction: Occasions and advantages of sin to be guarded against — The #smallcaps[seventh] direction: The first stirrings of sin to be vigorously opposed.]

==== Third Direction: Load the Conscience with Its Guilt

#smallcaps[This] is my #smallcaps[third] direction:

#emph[Load your conscience with the guilt of it.] Do not only consider that it has guilt, but load your conscience with the guilt of its actual outbreaks and disturbances.

To make the right use of this rule, I will give some particular directions:

1\. Follow #emph[God’s method] in it: begin with generals, and so come down to particulars.

(1.) Charge your conscience with the guilt that appears in this sin #emph[from the uprightness and holiness of the law]. Bring God’s holy law into your conscience, lay your corruption beside it, and pray that you may be affected by it. Consider the holiness of the law, its spirituality, its fiery severity, its inwardness, its absoluteness, and see how you can stand before it. Be much, I say, in affecting your conscience with the terror of the Lord in the law, and with how righteous it is that every one of your transgressions should receive a just recompense. Perhaps your conscience will invent shifts and evasions to ward off the force of this consideration. It may say that the condemning power of the law does not belong to you, that you are set free from it, and the like; and so, though you do not conform to it, you need not be so much troubled by it. But:

\[1.\] Tell your conscience that it cannot produce any evidence worth anything that you are free from the #emph[condemning] power of sin while your #emph[unmortified] lust lies in your heart. Perhaps, then, the law may make good its claim against you to full dominion, and then you are a lost creature. So it is best to weigh to the utmost what it has to say.

Assuredly, the man who pleads in the most secret reserve of his heart that he is freed from the condemning power of the law, so as secretly to excuse himself in giving the least allowance to any sin or lust, cannot produce on gospel grounds any evidence that gives tolerable spiritual security that he is in fact rightly freed from what he claims to be delivered from.

\[2.\] Whatever the #emph[outcome], the law has a commission from God to seize transgressors wherever it finds them, and to bring them before his throne, where they must plead for themselves. This is your case now: the law has found you out, and it will bring you before God. If you can plead a pardon, well and good; if not, the law will do its work.

\[3.\] In any case, this is the #emph[proper work] of the law: to uncover sin in its guilt, to awaken and humble the soul for it, to be a mirror that shows sin in its true colors. If you refuse to deal with the law on this account, it is not from faith, but from the hardness of your heart and the deceitfulness of sin.

This is a door through which too many professing Christians have gone out into open apostasy. They claimed such a deliverance from the law that they would no longer consult its guidance and direction; they would no longer measure their sin by it. Little by little, without their noticing, this principle passed from a notion in their heads to influence their practical judgment. Once it had taken possession there, it turned the will and the affections loose to every kind of abomination.

By such ways as these, then, I say, persuade your conscience to listen carefully to what the law says to you, in the name of the Lord, about your lust and corruption. Oh, if your ears are open, it will speak with a voice that will make you tremble, cast you to the ground, and fill you with astonishment. If you ever mean to mortify your corruptions, you must tie your conscience to the law and shut it off from all shifts and excuses, until it owns its guilt with a clear and thorough understanding; so that, as David says, your iniquity may be “always before” you.

(2.) Bring #emph[your lust to the gospel], not for relief, but for further conviction of its guilt. Look on him whom you have pierced, and be in bitterness. Say to your soul: “What have I done? What love, what mercy, what blood, what grace I have despised and trampled on! Is this how I repay the Father for his #emph[love], the Son for his #emph[blood], the Holy Spirit for his #emph[grace]? Is this how I repay the Lord? Have I defiled the heart that Christ died to wash, that the blessed Spirit has chosen to dwell in? And can I keep myself out of the dust? What can I say to the dear Lord Jesus? How shall I lift up my head with any boldness before him? Do I value communion with him so little that, for the sake of this vile lust, I have left him hardly any room in my heart? How shall I escape if I neglect such a great salvation? Meanwhile, what shall I say to the Lord? Love, mercy, grace, goodness, peace, joy, consolation: I have despised them all and counted them as nothing, so that I might harbor a lust in my heart. Have I been given a sight of God’s fatherly face, so that I might look on his face and provoke him to his face? Was my soul washed so that room might be made for new defilements? Shall I try to defeat the purpose of the death of Christ? Shall I daily grieve the Spirit by whom I am sealed for the day of redemption?” Keep your conscience occupied with this plea every day. See if it can stand before this aggravation of its guilt. If this does not make it sink and melt in some measure, I fear your case is dangerous.

2\. Come down to #emph[particulars]. Under the general head of the gospel all its benefits are to be considered, such as redemption, justification, and the like. So in particular, consider how the love in them has been brought home to your own soul, to aggravate the guilt of your corruption. For example:

(1.) Consider #emph[the infinite patience and forbearance] of God toward you in particular. Consider what advantages he might have taken against you, to make you a shame and a reproach in this world and an object of wrath forever. Consider how you have dealt treacherously and falsely with him time after time: you have flattered him with your lips, but broken all your promises and engagements, and that by means of the very sin you are now hunting down. Yet he has spared you time after time, although you seem to have boldly put it to the test how long he could hold out. And will you still sin against him? Will you still weary him, and burden him with your corruptions?

Have you not often been ready to conclude that it was utterly impossible for him to bear with you any longer; that he would cast you off and be gracious no more; that all his forbearance was used up, and hell and wrath were even now prepared for you? And yet, beyond all your expectation, he has returned with visits of love. And will you still go on provoking the eyes of his glory?

(2.) How often have you been #emph[at the very door of being hardened] by the deceitfulness of sin, and by the infinitely rich grace of God been brought back to communion with him again?

Have you not found grace decaying; delight in duties, ordinances, prayer and meditation vanishing; inclinations to loose, careless living thriving; and those who before were entangled almost beyond recovery? Have you not found yourself caught up, and that with delight, in ways, societies, and companions that God abhors? And will you venture to the brink of hardness any more?

(3.) All God’s gracious dealings with you in his providence (deliverances, afflictions, mercies, enjoyments) should all have their place here. By these, I say, and similar means, load your conscience. Do not leave it until it is thoroughly affected with the guilt of your indwelling corruption, until it feels its wound and lies in the dust before the Lord. Unless this is done in earnest, all other efforts are in vain. As long as the conscience has any means to lighten the guilt of sin, the soul will never vigorously attempt its mortification.

==== Fourth Direction: A Constant Longing for Deliverance

#smallcaps[Fourth], being thus affected with your sin, next get #emph[a constant longing and panting after deliverance from its power]. Do not let your heart be content for one moment with its present frame and condition. In natural and civil things, longing desires for anything are of no value or account except as far as they stir up the person who has them to a diligent use of means to bring about what he aims at. In spiritual things it is otherwise. Longing, yearning, and panting after deliverance is a grace in itself, and it has a mighty power to conform the soul to the likeness of the thing longed for. So the apostle, describing the repentance and godly sorrow of the Corinthians, counts this as one eminent grace then set to work: “what longing” (2 Cor 7:11). And in this case of indwelling sin and its power, what frame does he say he is in? (Rom 7:24). His heart breaks out with longing into a most passionate expression of desire for deliverance. Now, if this is the frame of the saints when they consider indwelling sin in general, how much more must it be heightened and increased when the troubling rage and power of some particular lust and corruption is added to it! Be sure of this: unless you #emph[long] for deliverance, you will not have it.

This will make the heart watch for every chance to gain an advantage over its enemy, and ready to take hold of any help it is given to destroy it. Strong desires are the very life of the “praying at all times” that we are commanded in every condition, and in none is it more necessary than in this. They set faith and hope to work; they are the soul’s reaching out after the Lord.

So get your heart into a panting, longing frame: long for it, sigh for it, cry out for it. You#super[+] know David’s example; I need not dwell on it.

==== Fifth Direction: Consider Whether It Is Rooted in the Nature

The #smallcaps[fifth] direction is this:

Consider whether the disorder that troubles you is not rooted in your #emph[nature], and cherished, inflamed, and heightened by your #emph[constitution]. A tendency to some sins may no doubt lie in the natural temperament and disposition of men. In this case consider:

1\. This does not in the least #emph[lessen] the guilt of your sin. Some, with open profaneness, will blame gross enormities on their temperament and disposition; and whether others may not relieve themselves of the pressing guilt of their disorders by the same consideration, I do not know. It is from the fall, from the original corruption of our natures, that the #emph[fomes] [tinder] and nourishment of any sin lives on in our natural temperament. David counts his being brought forth in iniquity and conceived in sin#footnote[Ps 51:5.] as an aggravation of the sin that followed, not a lessening or excuse of it. That you are especially inclined to some sinful disorder is only a special outbreak of original lust in your nature, which should especially abase and humble you.

2\. What you must #emph[fix] on here, as regards your walk with God, is this: so great an advantage is given to sin, and to Satan too, by your temperament and disposition, that without extraordinary watchfulness, care, and diligence they will certainly prevail against your soul. Thousands have been hurried headlong to hell on this account who otherwise might at least have gone at a gentler, less provoking, less harmful pace.

3\. For the mortification of any disorder so rooted in a man’s nature, besides all the other ways and means already named or still to be discussed, there is one #emph[remedy] especially suited to it. It is that of the apostle: “I discipline my body and make it my slave” (1 Cor 9:27). Bringing the body itself into subjection is an ordinance of God that tends to the mortification of sin. It checks the natural root of the disorder and withers it by taking away the richness of its soil. The Papists, men ignorant of the righteousness of Christ, of the work of his Spirit, and of the whole business in hand, have laid the whole weight and stress of mortification on voluntary services and penances that lead to the subjection of the body, knowing the true nature neither of sin nor of mortification. Perhaps because of this, it may on the other side be a temptation to some to neglect means of humiliation that God himself owns and has appointed. Bringing the body into subjection in the case under discussion, by cutting short the natural appetite, by fasting, by keeping vigils, and the like, is no doubt acceptable to God, provided it is done within the following limits:

(1.) The outward weakening and wearing down of the body must not be looked on as a good thing in itself, or as if any mortification consisted in it; that would bring us back under fleshly ordinances. It is only a means to the end proposed: the weakening of a disorder in its natural root and seat. A man may have a lean body and a lean soul together.

(2.) The means by which this is done, fasting, keeping vigils, and the like, must not be looked on as things that can produce true mortification of any sin in themselves and by their own power. If they could, sin might be mortified in any unregenerate person in the world without any help of the Spirit. They are to be looked on only as ways by which the Spirit may, and sometimes does, put forth his strength to accomplish his own work, especially in the case mentioned. A lack of right understanding and due use of these and similar considerations has produced among the Papists a mortification better suited to horses and other beasts of the field than to believers.

This is the sum of what has been said: when the disorder we complain of seems to be rooted in our natural temperament and constitution, then, while we apply our souls to share in the blood and Spirit of Christ, we must also try to check the natural root of that disorder in God’s way.

==== Sixth Direction: Watch Against Its Occasions and Advantages

The #smallcaps[sixth] direction is this:

Consider what #emph[occasions] and what #emph[advantages] your disorder has taken to exert and show itself, and watch against them all.

This is one part of the duty our blessed Savior commends to his disciples under the name of #emph[watching]: “What I say to you#super[+], I say to everyone: Keep watch!” (Mark 13:37). In Luke 21:34 it is, “Watch yourselves, or your#super[+] hearts will be weighed down.” Watch against every outbreak of your corruptions. I mean the duty David professed himself to be exercised in: “I have kept myself from iniquity,” he says. He watched all the ways and workings of his iniquity, to head them off and to rise up against them. This is what we are called to under the name of “considering our ways.” Consider what ways, what companions, what opportunities, what studies, what business, what conditions have at any time given, or usually give, advantages to your disorders, and set yourself carefully against them all. Men will do this with their bodily weaknesses and disorders. The seasons, the diet, the air that have proved harmful will be avoided. Are the things of the soul of less importance? Know that the man who dares to toy with occasions of sin will dare to sin. He who will venture on temptations to wickedness will venture on wickedness. Hazael thought he would never be as wicked as the prophet told him he would be. To convince him, the prophet tells him no more than this: “You will be king over Aram.” If he will venture on temptations to cruelty, he will be cruel. Tell a man he will commit such and such sins, and he will be startled at it. If you can convince him that he will venture on the occasions and temptations to them, he will have little ground left for his confidence. There are many particular directions belonging to this head, which I will not go into now. But because this head is of no less importance than the whole doctrine treated here, I have dealt with it at length in another treatise, on entering into temptation.

==== Seventh Direction: Rise Mightily Against Its First Stirrings

The #smallcaps[seventh] direction is this:

Rise up mightily against #emph[the first stirrings] of your disorder, its first conceptions; do not let it gain the least ground. Do not say, “This far it shall go, and no farther.” If it is allowed one step, it will take another. It is impossible to set bounds to sin. It is like water in a channel: if it once breaks out, it will have its course. It is easier to keep it from acting at all than to keep it within bounds. That is why James sets out the steps and progress of lust (ch. 1:14, 15), so that we may stop it at the entrance. Do you find your corruption beginning to entangle your thoughts? Rise up against it with all your strength, with no less indignation than if it had fully accomplished what it aims at. Consider what an unclean thought would have: it would have you roll yourself in folly and filth. Ask #emph[envy] what it would have: #emph[murder] and #emph[destruction] are at the end of it. Set yourself against it with no less vigor than if it had utterly debased you into wickedness. Without this course you will not prevail. As sin gains ground in the affections, so that they delight in it, it gains ground also on the understanding, so that it makes light of it.
